  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nRadar remains generally quiet over the forecast area this \nafternoon, with a few isolated showers popping up within passing\ncumulus fields. At the time of writing, a cold front was \nanalyzed to be draped through the Great Lakes and down into Ohio\nand Indiana. Hi-res models paint a cluster of storms \ninitializing over the Ohio Valley within the next hour of this \ndiscussion issuance, then developing into a line oriented from \nnortheast to southwest that will propagate into New England and \nthe Mid-Atlantic through the course of this evening. \n\nThe southern tail of this line clips our southeast Ohio and \nnorthern West Virginia zones near dusk, losing aid from daytime \nheating and yielding a weakening trend as the line progresses \neastward. Main changes made to the severe weather outlook this \nafternoon took place outside of our forecast area, with an \nupgrade to a slight risk for the northern WV panhandle into \nPennsylvania and New York. While the strongest storms are slated\nto occur north of the area, enough shear will be present that if\nstorms maintain their composure as they arrive into southeastern\nOhio, isolated damaging wind gusts and hail could be observed.\nPrecipitation tracks into the mountains late tonight into early\nMonday morning with the cold front, tapering off during the\npre-dawn hours once the boundary completes its passage through\nthe area.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nIn the wake of the frontal passage, a building ridge is slated\nto amplify over the Tennessee and Ohio Valleys beginning on\nMonday through at least midweek. Hot and humid conditions\ndeveloping within this region may bolster a few diurnally driven\nshowers and storms during peak heating hours, but otherwise\nlooking like a relatively dry forecast for the upcoming work\nweek. The aforementioned heat that builds within this upper\nlevel regime advertises the return of heat indices inching\ncloser to the triple digit mark by midweek. This will likely\nbe more the case for urbanized areas across the Kanawha Valley,\nbut the majority of the lowlands will reach mid to upper 90s \nheat index values for Wednesday and Thursday afternoons.\n\nThe ridge breaks down over the Central Appalachians by the end\nof the week into next weekend, opening the door for waves of\nshortwaves to journey through the Ohio Valley. Daily\nopportunities for showers and storms resume as a result.",
